# The doors corpus (`M203` `S3`) — the project behind the door gate, and **it is never run**.
#
# WHY IT IS A SEPARATE PROJECT FROM `../project/`. That one is *executed*: `make-fixtures.mjs`
# drives it against `server.mjs` under each env and keeps the report directories as
# `ui-page.test.ts`'s oracle, and its own config calls it "small on purpose". The claim this corpus
# carries — which lenses a test is in, and therefore which door shows it — is a claim about
# `lensesOfTest`, `lensesOfCrawl` and `readProject`, all pure functions of the AST. It needs no
# server, no report and no run to be true, so giving it one would buy a slower gate and a wider
# oracle for nothing.
#
# It is `printer-corpus/`'s idiom, applied to a different claim: a corpus authored **for** an
# assertion rather than harvested and hoped over (`D1056`, `M201` §2.1).
#
# **It is not an example project and must not be promoted into one.** An example is runnable by
# definition; this is not. `M202` §7 names the example as its own artifact for exactly this reason.
#
# The `authorized target` below exists so `crawl.tflw` checks clean (`TF060`). It names a port
# nothing listens on, deliberately — a corpus that is never run cannot need a reachable one.
defaults
  authorized target "http://127.0.0.1:4719" reason "the doors corpus is parsed, never run — see this file's header"

env local default
  api "http://127.0.0.1:4719"
  web "http://127.0.0.1:4719"
